
# Google Meet 

Google Meet records videos at a variable frame rate, which can cause playback and editing issues in Blender. Blender works best with constant-frame-rate video, where frames are captured at evenly spaced intervals. Google Meet may vary the time between frames based on network and performance conditions, resulting in a variable-frame-rate video file.

In such cases, it is recommended to convert the video to a constant-frame-rate format before importing it into Blender. This can be done using video conversion tools like FFmpeg, which allows you to specify a constant frame rate for the output video.

For a typical Meet recording, **30 fps** is a sensible default for a YouTube video and is widely supported. However, Meet recordings can vary, so check the source's frame-rate information. 

```bash
ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -fps_mode cfr -r 30 -c:v libx264 -crf 18 -preset fast -c:a aac -b:a 192k output_cfr.mp4
```

## Verify a video has a constant frame rate

A constant frame rate video will have only one unique frame duration. The following command uses `ffprobe` to list all unique frame durations in the video. If the output shows a single value, the video has a constant frame rate. Multiple values indicate a variable frame rate.

```bash
ffprobe -v error -select_streams v:0 -show_entries frame=pkt_duration_time -of csv=p=0 output_cfr.mp4 | sort -u
```

## Extract the shared screen and participants videos into two separate files

Use the script to to1080p.sh to convert the video to 1080p resolution before extracting the shared screen and participants videos into separate files.

```bash
./to1080p.sh input.mp4 output_1080p.mp4
```

Then use the crop2.sh script to extract the shared screen and participants videos into separate files.

```bash
./crop2.sh output_1080p.mp4 shared_screen.mp4 participants.mp4
```

